---
/**
 * THE BRAND LOCKUP. Single source of truth for the wordmark.
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS (2026-08-03): the wordmark was hardcoded in three places
 * with two different stylesheets, and they had drifted. The site header and
 * footer rendered "Save My Gig!" at weight 900 with -0.02em tracking; the
 * emergency tunnel rendered "SAVE MY GIG" at weight 800 with +0.01em tracking
 * from global.css, no tagline, no accent, and it never received the
 * exclamation mark the rest of the site carried. Antonio saw the difference
 * on production and was right: nobody designs a brand at two weights with
 * opposite tracking on purpose. That is drift, not a variant.
 *
 * Now: one component, one treatment, three sizes. Change the lettering here
 * and it propagates to the header, the footer and the rescue tunnel at once.
 *
 * ANTONIO'S LETTERING SPEC (2026-08-03): "SAVE MY GIG", GIG in the brand red,
 * NO exclamation mark, and in the full variant the wordmark spans exactly the
 * width of "DJ BOOTH INTELLIGENCE" beneath it so the lockup reads as one
 * rectangle. The sizes below are MEASURED against the tagline at each
 * breakpoint, not guessed. Change the tagline and you must re-measure.
 *
 * THE TAGLINE CHANGED 2026-08-03: "DJ rescue and prevention" -> "DJ Booth
 * Intelligence". His call, and the reasoning is worth keeping because it
 * governs any future version:
 *   - The old one was REDUNDANT. "Save My Gig" already means rescue, so the
 *     tagline repeated the name in the most valuable space on the site,
 *     printed on all 107 pages.
 *   - The new one divides the work: the NAME carries urgency, the TAGLINE
 *     carries depth. Together they claim a category rather than describing a
 *     service, and the site can now back that claim (60-term dictionary,
 *     firmware matrix, eight hardware references, thirteen fix guides).
 *   - It sets up the book. Antonio's "DJ Booth Bible" ships in about two
 *     months, so "Booth" becomes the word the brand owns across both, and
 *     each one sells the other. This is why "Intelligence" is not an AI
 *     claim: it is the field-intelligence sense, anchored to a physical
 *     place by "Booth".
 * NOTE the tagline is SHORTER than the old one (21 characters against 24),
 * so every letter-spacing value below had to grow to keep the rectangle. They
 * are measured, not estimated. Verified equal to within 1px at every
 * breakpoint in both variants.
 *
 * NOT fixable here: the wordmark is also baked into logo-shield.svg,
 * seal-footer.webp, save-my-gig-seal.png, og-share.jpg and the 13 per-article
 * OG cards, all of which still read "SAVE MY GIG!". Those need an image
 * re-export pass.
 *
 * TWO VARIANTS, TWO WIDTH REFERENCES, DELIBERATELY (2026-08-03).
 * A wordmark has to be measured against whatever sits under it, and the two
 * surfaces do not have the same thing under them:
 *   full    the site header AND the rescue tunnel bar. Reference is the
 *           tagline.
 *   footer  the site footer. Reference is the ROW OF FOUR SOCIAL BUTTONS,
 *           because Antonio's rectangle rule says the wordmark, the tagline
 *           and the social row are ONE width. Folding the footer onto the
 *           `full` sizes is exactly what broke it: the footer inherited a
 *           lockup measured against the wrong object and came out 23px short
 *           on desktop and 32px long between 401 and 414. Same lettering,
 *           same weight, same tracking, same red MY. Only the SIZE differs,
 *           because only the reference differs.
 * This is not drift. Drift is two things that should match and do not. These
 * are measured to two different objects on purpose.
 *
 * `compact` DELETED 2026-08-03. It existed for the rescue top bar, at a
 * 1.02rem wordmark beside a 22.5x26 shield with no tagline, against the site
 * header's 1.395rem beside 47x54 with one. Antonio, on staging: "the upper
 * part is still looking very bad, it looks really bad, this is like this in
 * many pages." He was right and the measurement was worse than it looked:
 * the rescue bar was nearly the same HEIGHT as the site header while the
 * mark inside it was less than half the size, so it read as a full bar with
 * a small logo floating in it. The justification on file was that nothing in
 * the tunnel may compete with the decision on screen. That argument holds for
 * the NAV, which stays out. It never held for the brand, because shrinking
 * the mark bought no space back. One wordmark size for every header now.
 */
interface Props {
  variant?: 'full' | 'footer';
}
const { variant = 'full' } = Astro.props;
const cls = variant === 'footer' ? 'smg-foot' : 'smg-full';

// THE TAGLINE IS ALIGNED BY CONSTRUCTION, NOT BY MEASUREMENT (2026-08-03).
// The first approach set a letter-spacing value solved against painted pixels
// in headless Chromium, verified to 0.00px, and Antonio's production
// screenshot STILL showed the E ending 2px past the G. Same font file,
// different engine: macOS and Linux round glyph advances differently, and
// over 21 letters the rounding drift accumulates. A constant solved on one
// machine can never guarantee another, and it silently breaks again the day
// the tagline text changes.
// So the tagline is now one flex row, one span per letter, with
// justify-content: space-between. The FIRST letter is pinned to the lockup's
// left edge and the LAST letter to its right edge by layout, and the browser
// distributes the gaps itself, exactly, on every OS at every zoom. Spaces
// become nbsp spans so word gaps stay visibly wider than letter gaps.
// Change TAG below to anything and the alignment holds with no re-measuring.
const TAG = 'DJ Booth Intelligence';
const tagChars = [...TAG].map((c) => (c === ' ' ? ' ' : c));
---
<span class:list={['smg-brand', cls]}>
  <span class="smg-word">Save My <span class="smg-accent">Gig</span></span>
  {/* Letter spans are hidden from assistive tech; the sr-only span carries
      the real string so a screen reader hears words, not 21 letters. */}
  <span class="smg-tag">
    <span class="sr-only">{TAG}</span>
    <span class="smg-tag-row" aria-hidden="true">{tagChars.map((c) => <span>{c}</span>)}</span>
  </span>
</span>

<style is:global>
  .smg-brand { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 3px; line-height: 1; }
  .smg-word {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 900;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--text);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  /* THE ONE PIECE OF COLOUR IN THE MARK, and it moved 2026-08-03.
     It was on "My" (Save white, MY red, Gig white). Antonio asked to see the
     alternatives side by side at real size and chose GIG.
     Why it is better: the accent now lands at the END, so the lockup
     finishes on the red instead of being interrupted mid-phrase. The old
     arrangement split the mark into three chunks, white red white, and the
     eye stopped twice. It also holds together better small, because a middle
     accent leaves two white islands either side of the red while an end
     accent does not, and the mark lives small in the mobile header.
     Named for its ROLE, not its content: it was `.smg-my`, which would have
     been a lie the moment the accent moved, and did not survive one move. */
  .smg-accent { color: var(--red); }
  /* NO letter-spacing and NO width numbers here, on purpose. The tagline's
     edges are pinned to the wordmark by the flex row below, so the rectangle
     is exact by construction on every OS, every zoom, every future wording.
     The frontmatter comment carries the full story: a solved letter-spacing
     measured 0.00px in headless Chromium and still drifted 2px on Antonio's
     Mac, because font engines round glyph advances differently. Never bring
     letter-spacing back to hit a width. */
  .smg-tag {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-body); font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--text-dim); white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .smg-tag-row { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; }
  /* Side-bearing compensation, the ONLY constants left in the alignment.
     Flex pins the letter BOXES to the edges; the visible ink sits a hair
     inside a box (a glyph's side bearing). The Archivo G carries more right
     bearing than the Inter E, so without this the E's ink ended 1px past the
     G's. Bearings are metrics inside the font file, identical on every OS
     and engine, so unlike the letter-spacing these constants CANNOT drift
     between my render and Antonio's screen. Measured per variant against
     painted ink at 1x, 2x and 3x. */
  /* padding-left 0.5px: Antonio's falcon-eye report against a ~6x zoom
     screenshot, the D's ink starting a hair left of the S's. Half a pixel is
     the smallest honest step at this size; it moves ONLY the first letter,
     space-between re-absorbs it, the E stays pinned. */
  .smg-full .smg-tag-row { padding-left: 0.5px; padding-right: 1px; }
  .smg-foot .smg-tag-row { padding-left: 0.5px; padding-right: 0.5px; }

  /* FULL: header (site and rescue bar). Only the SIZES live here now. */
  .smg-full .smg-word { font-size: 1.395rem; }
  .smg-full .smg-tag { font-size: 0.55rem; }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .smg-full .smg-word { font-size: 1.636rem; }
    .smg-full .smg-tag { font-size: 0.62rem; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 400px) {
    .smg-full .smg-word { font-size: 1.332rem; }
    .smg-full .smg-tag { font-size: 0.55rem; }
  }

  /* FOOTER: the wordmark is sized to the SOCIAL ROW (Antonio's rectangle
     rule: wordmark, tagline and social row are ONE width). The tagline no
     longer needs its own numbers, it stretches to the wordmark.
     If you change the social button size or gap, re-measure the WORD sizes. */
  .smg-foot .smg-word { font-size: 1.605rem; }
  .smg-foot .smg-tag { font-size: 0.55rem; }
  @media (max-width: 414px) {
    .smg-foot .smg-word { font-size: 1.331rem; }
    .smg-foot .smg-tag { font-size: 0.45rem; }
  }

  /* No third size. The rescue bar uses `full`, same as the site header. */
</style>
